{"name":"Cottage Walk","hex":"#a08e77","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cottage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small house.","A seasonal home of any size or stature, a recreational home or a home in a remote location."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stay at a seasonal home, to go cottaging.","To have homosexual sex in a public lavatory; to practice cottaging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cottage"},{"term":"walk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move on the feet by alternately setting each foot (or pair or group of feet: in the case of some non-human animals with four or more feet) forward, with at least one foot on the ground at all times. Compare run.","To \"walk free\", i.e. to win, or avoid, a criminal court case, particularly when actually guilty."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trip made by walking.","A distance walked."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/walk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cottage","blocks":[{"text":"A cottage during England's feudal period was the holding by a cottager of a small house with enough garden to feed a family and in return for the cottage, the cottager had to provide some form of service to the manorial lord. However, in time cottage just became the general term for a small house. In modern usage, a cottage is usually a modest, often cosy dwelling, typically in a rural or semi-rural location and not necessarily in England. The cottage orné, often quite large and grand residences built by the nobility, dates back to a movement of \"rustic\" stylised cottages of the late 18th and early 19th century during the Romantic movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottage","wikidata":"Q5783996"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A cottage on Inch Island, Ireland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACountry_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg/500px-South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg/500px-South_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A typical cottage in Devon, with walls built of cob and a thatched roof","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASouth_Devon_cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_81799.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg/500px-Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg/500px-Miners_Cottages%2C_Pit_Village%2C_Beamish_Museum%2C_5_September_2013.jpg?ut